raw.body:
Just found your question because I have similar situation: I want to guarantee the loading of lazy slice before the thunk will be fulfilled...
So I research for your problem too. If I understand you right you call thunk always (so there is no optimization in number of calls). And then you decide what the site to load (with or without slice) depending on the received data. If so seems you can use dynamic import of the slice in thunk, before fulfil it:
```js
export const thunk = createAsyncThunk('typePrefix', async (params)=>{
  try {
    // ...
    const data = await request()
    if(data.success){
      await import('/path/to/slice.js') // <-- Here your slice will be requested and loaded (but you will lose some time for loading)
      return data // <-- Fulfil the thunk
    }
    // ...
  }
  // ...
})
```
Maybe something is out of my sight, but I tested and it works in my project.

P.s. And I realised it's not a risk for me (and there is no problem at all), because I import selector from slice always before thunk call.

> According to [docs](https://redux.js.org/usage/code-splitting#selector-utilities) your selector for lazy slice will return initial data even if slice does not persist in the store. There shouldn't be error on calling selector before slice comes to the store.

Correct, RTK includes two methods for wrapping selectors to ensure slice state is available:
```js
const rootReducer = combineSlices(authSlice).withLazyLoadedSlices<{ posts: PostState }>();

// slice first approach
const postSlice = createSlice({
  name: "posts",
  initialState: { items: [] },
  reducers: {},
  selectors: {
    selectPosts: (state) => state.items
  }
})

postSlice.selectors.selectPosts({}) // throws, no post slice

const injectedPostSlice = postSlice.injectInto(rootReducer) 

injectedPostSlice.selectors.selectPosts({}) // calls selector with initial state if no post slice

// rootReducer first approach
const selectPosts = rootReducer.selector(state => state.posts.items)

rootReducer.inject(postSlice);

// uses a proxy around state to provide initial state for any reducers that have been injected but aren't in state yet
selectPosts({})
```

Well, now I see. You have got all the data in your initial request for all possible slices and sites. And want to optimize loading and creating of slices (which is actually a JS object with some "functional" sugar = not so expensive at least). Even if you will (then) use only one site (from your handful) and 90% (e.g.) of data is not needed, you must store it or refetch every time you need it (there is no magic, it's a price for such solution). Seems this storing is more expensive than creating another one slice. 
Back to your title question: put RTK allows such feature and you will have active reducer before slice is created, it must store the data somewhere (in a JS object?). If you will never need the slice, data will be stored permanent (actually, though named "temporary until slice is created"). What is the difference with creation of slice at the moment of receiving of the data? Rhetorical question...
But OK, it's your app, your decision whatever you (not me) did it and are responsible for the app's stability and developing!  
So you want to request the initial data once for all slices and have to keep it until it will be used. Here I see something like this solution:
Store initial data at closure (initialData const, which you use at slice creation) or even at session storage and then put it in lazy-loaded slice with `initialState` property with const or function of `createSlice()`.
```js
// lazySlice.js
import store from 'store';
const initialState = ()=>{
  const necessaryData = getStoredData() // get from session, closure etc.
  // if you want to get data from main slice and put in site's one, you have to do it in an 'init' thunk with further cleanup at main... but i didn't test it
  return necessaryData
}

const lazySlice = createSlice({
  name: 'lazy',
  initialState,
  // ...
})
```
But all such solutions are just putting same thing at another place. I can't figure any performance or other gains. Making a number of closured consts you get an alternative store managed by you. The only reason i saw is your desire to lightweight core slice, again, just managing internal structure — I don't know the boundaries of acceptable modifications you can do in your app and architecture. Also you can create just separate slice with initial data and don't join it with core slice. At the end of the day if you need to store data you have to store it. And seems we are talking longer than implement any of the cases)))))))
Have a good time and a good luck with your project!
